1
00:00:00,275 --> 00:00:04,150
This is a real language model, a tiny one, being trained with reinforcement

2
00:00:04,150 --> 00:00:05,038
learning.

3
00:00:05,038 --> 00:00:10,788
Every possible answer it could give to 5675 plus 5563 is drawn here as a river

4
00:00:10,788 --> 00:00:14,525
of probability, flowing left to right one token at a time.

5
00:00:14,525 --> 00:00:15,938
Green endings are right;

6
00:00:15,938 --> 00:00:17,462
red endings are wrong.

7
00:00:17,462 --> 00:00:19,775
Watch the river as training proceeds.

8
00:00:19,775 --> 00:00:22,838
Nobody shows the model a single correct answer.

9
00:00:22,838 --> 00:00:24,862
Each attempt gets back one number:

10
00:00:24,862 --> 00:00:27,675
one if it was right, zero if it wasn't.

11
00:00:27,675 --> 00:00:31,175
And yet the river bends toward green.

12
00:00:31,808 --> 00:00:35,896
The same idea, at scale, is how today's reasoning models are made.

13
00:00:35,896 --> 00:00:40,758
In 2025, DeepSeek applied it directly to a base model, rewarding nothing but

14
00:00:40,758 --> 00:00:43,458
correct final answers in the right format.

15
00:00:43,458 --> 00:00:47,708
Its score on a hard math competition went from sixteen percent to seventy-eight,

16
00:00:47,708 --> 00:00:51,446
and its answers grew from hundreds of tokens to thousands, as it learned to

17
00:00:51,446 --> 00:00:52,971
check its own work.

18
00:00:52,971 --> 00:00:56,146
OpenAI had reported the same trend for o1:

19
00:00:56,146 --> 00:01:00,383
more reinforcement learning, better reasoning.

20
00:01:00,842 --> 00:01:04,367
How can a single bit per attempt possibly train a network with millions of

21
00:01:04,367 --> 00:01:05,667
parameters?

22
00:01:05,667 --> 00:01:10,042
The answer is a short chain of mathematical ideas, and at the end of the chain

23
00:01:10,042 --> 00:01:11,467
is this formula:

24
00:01:11,467 --> 00:01:15,867
GRPO, the objective behind most open reasoning models.

25
00:01:15,867 --> 00:01:17,479
It looks intimidating.

26
00:01:17,479 --> 00:01:21,479
By the end of this video, every symbol in it will have been derived, along

27
00:01:21,479 --> 00:01:25,467
with the reasons the labs keep changing it.

28
00:01:25,908 --> 00:01:27,033
Here's the route.

29
00:01:27,033 --> 00:01:30,896
We'll derive the policy gradient from one identity, then learn how to take

30
00:01:30,896 --> 00:01:32,333
safe steps with it.

31
00:01:32,333 --> 00:01:36,608
We'll see how human preferences become rewards, and solve for the best possible

32
00:01:36,608 --> 00:01:38,283
policy exactly.

33
00:01:38,283 --> 00:01:43,646
Then GRPO and its hidden biases, and finally what changes when this runs on

34
00:01:43,646 --> 00:01:45,308
thousands of GPUs.

35
00:01:51,065 --> 00:01:52,878
First, the translation.

36
00:01:52,878 --> 00:01:56,440
To reinforcement learning, a language model is a policy.

37
00:01:56,440 --> 00:01:58,690
The state is everything written so far:

38
00:01:58,690 --> 00:02:01,540
the prompt and the answer's first few tokens.

39
00:02:01,540 --> 00:02:05,290
The action is the next token, drawn from the model's softmax.

40
00:02:05,290 --> 00:02:07,053
The environment couldn't be simpler:

41
00:02:07,053 --> 00:02:08,515
it appends the token.

42
00:02:08,515 --> 00:02:11,178
When the answer ends, a reward arrives.

43
00:02:11,178 --> 00:02:15,690
Everything else in this video is about that last line.

44
00:02:16,157 --> 00:02:19,845
Here is the whole space of answers for one prompt, as the real model sees

45
00:02:19,845 --> 00:02:20,470
it.

46
00:02:20,470 --> 00:02:24,370
Reading left to right, each column is a token, and each block's height is

47
00:02:24,370 --> 00:02:26,920
the probability of reaching that prefix.

48
00:02:26,920 --> 00:02:29,795
The river splits wherever the model is unsure.

49
00:02:29,795 --> 00:02:33,532
The top stream shows its work, writing the sum backwards first;

50
00:02:33,532 --> 00:02:36,907
the rest answer directly, and fork at every carry.

51
00:02:36,907 --> 00:02:40,995
The probability of a whole answer is the product of its tokens' probabilities,

52
00:02:40,995 --> 00:02:43,632
along its path.

53
00:02:43,765 --> 00:02:46,940
The checker marks each complete answer right or wrong:

54
00:02:46,940 --> 00:02:48,415
this last column.

55
00:02:48,415 --> 00:02:52,603
With a reward of one for right and zero for wrong, the expected reward is

56
00:02:52,603 --> 00:02:55,590
simply the total probability of the right answers:

57
00:02:55,590 --> 00:02:57,690
the green part of that column.

58
00:02:57,690 --> 00:03:00,703
Reinforcement learning, for us, means one thing:

59
00:03:00,703 --> 00:03:05,340
reshape the river so that more of it ends in green.

60
00:03:05,832 --> 00:03:07,907
Meet the model behind that picture.

61
00:03:07,907 --> 00:03:12,157
It's a pocket-sized transformer, three layers deep, pretrained on text full

62
00:03:12,157 --> 00:03:13,970
of four-digit additions.

63
00:03:13,970 --> 00:03:16,307
A quarter of the examples show their work:

64
00:03:16,307 --> 00:03:21,020
the sum written right to left, digit by digit with its carries, then the answer.

65
00:03:21,020 --> 00:03:25,532
The rest answer at once, and like people doing sums in their heads, they sometimes

66
00:03:25,532 --> 00:03:26,870
forget to carry.

67
00:03:26,870 --> 00:03:29,757
The model learned both habits faithfully.

68
00:03:29,757 --> 00:03:34,370
It shows its work about a quarter of the time and is then almost always right;

69
00:03:34,370 --> 00:03:36,832
otherwise it slips like its teachers.

70
00:03:36,832 --> 00:03:38,082
We'll call it the adder.

71
00:03:40,072 --> 00:03:43,372
Here is the problem reinforcement learning has to solve.

72
00:03:43,372 --> 00:03:45,922
The parameters theta define a policy.

73
00:03:45,922 --> 00:03:49,547
We sample an answer from it, and a checker hands back a reward.

74
00:03:49,547 --> 00:03:53,547
We want to make the expected reward bigger, so we want its gradient with respect

75
00:03:53,547 --> 00:03:54,547
to theta.

76
00:03:54,547 --> 00:03:56,885
But backpropagation can't get there.

77
00:03:56,885 --> 00:04:01,097
Sampling is a discrete choice, and the checker might be a test suite running

78
00:04:01,097 --> 00:04:02,247
in a sandbox:

79
00:04:02,247 --> 00:04:05,572
there is no derivative to follow.

80
00:04:06,047 --> 00:04:10,272
The way around it is one of the most useful identities in machine learning.

81
00:04:10,272 --> 00:04:15,260
Write the expected reward as a sum over every possible answer y, of its probability

82
00:04:15,260 --> 00:04:16,835
times its reward.

83
00:04:16,835 --> 00:04:21,672
The reward doesn't depend on theta, so the gradient only touches the probability.

84
00:04:21,672 --> 00:04:24,222
Now multiply and divide by pi:

85
00:04:24,222 --> 00:04:28,447
the gradient of pi equals pi times the gradient of log pi.

86
00:04:28,447 --> 00:04:33,022
And a sum weighted by pi is just an average over samples from pi.

87
00:04:33,022 --> 00:04:36,685
So we can estimate the gradient without differentiating through anything we

88
00:04:36,685 --> 00:04:37,747
can't:

89
00:04:37,747 --> 00:04:42,647
sample answers, score them, and average reward times the gradient of the log

90
00:04:42,647 --> 00:04:44,297
probability.

91
00:04:44,297 --> 00:04:48,247
That gradient of log pi has a name, the score function.

92
00:04:48,247 --> 00:04:53,747
And this estimator, published by Ronald Williams in 1992 as REINFORCE, is

93
00:04:53,747 --> 00:04:57,847
the seed of every algorithm in this video.

94
00:04:58,356 --> 00:05:01,781
To see what this does, shrink the problem to one dimension.

95
00:05:01,781 --> 00:05:05,881
The policy picks a number a from a bell curve centered at mu, and the reward

96
00:05:05,881 --> 00:05:08,268
is this landscape with two hills.

97
00:05:08,268 --> 00:05:09,981
Sample ten actions.

98
00:05:09,981 --> 00:05:13,168
For a bell curve, the score is simply a minus mu:

99
00:05:13,168 --> 00:05:18,066
each sample says move toward me, in proportion to how far away I am.

100
00:05:18,066 --> 00:05:21,328
On their own, these pushes cancel out on average.

101
00:05:21,328 --> 00:05:24,053
In fact the score always has mean zero:

102
00:05:24,053 --> 00:05:28,366
its average is the gradient of the total probability, and the total is always

103
00:05:28,366 --> 00:05:29,228
one.

104
00:05:29,228 --> 00:05:30,216
Remember this;

105
00:05:30,216 --> 00:05:33,141
it will matter in a minute.

106
00:05:33,366 --> 00:05:36,653
Now weight each push by the reward its sample earned.

107
00:05:36,653 --> 00:05:39,453
Samples near the tall hill keep their arrows;

108
00:05:39,453 --> 00:05:41,541
samples in the valley lose theirs.

109
00:05:41,541 --> 00:05:45,803
The symmetry breaks, and the average points toward higher reward.

110
00:05:45,803 --> 00:05:50,266
Take a small step along that average, sample again, and repeat.

111
00:05:50,266 --> 00:05:54,241
The bell curve climbs the hill, and nobody ever computed the derivative of

112
00:05:54,241 --> 00:05:56,578
the reward.

113
00:05:57,045 --> 00:06:01,607
For a language model the answer is a sequence of tokens, and its log probability

114
00:06:01,607 --> 00:06:04,257
is a sum, one term per token.

115
00:06:04,257 --> 00:06:06,370
So the policy gradient becomes:

116
00:06:06,370 --> 00:06:10,257
the answer's reward times the sum of every token's score.

117
00:06:10,257 --> 00:06:15,145
Each token of a sampled answer gets pushed up, or down, by the same amount:

118
00:06:15,145 --> 00:06:17,503
the reward of the whole answer.

119
00:06:17,503 --> 00:06:20,153
Compare that with supervised fine-tuning.

120
00:06:20,153 --> 00:06:24,778
There, you maximize the log probability of a reference answer someone wrote.

121
00:06:24,778 --> 00:06:29,116
Here, you do the same thing to the model's own answer, weighted by how well

122
00:06:29,116 --> 00:06:30,128
it did.

123
00:06:30,128 --> 00:06:35,728
Reinforcement learning is fine-tuning on your own samples, graded.

124
00:06:36,278 --> 00:06:39,066
What does pushing up one token actually do?

125
00:06:39,066 --> 00:06:43,316
Inside the model, the next token comes from a softmax over logits.

126
00:06:43,316 --> 00:06:47,728
The score of the sampled token, with respect to the logits, is one minus its

127
00:06:47,728 --> 00:06:51,978
probability for the sampled token, and minus the probability for every other

128
00:06:51,978 --> 00:06:53,041
token.

129
00:06:53,041 --> 00:06:57,678
So a positive reward raises the sampled token and takes mass from the others,

130
00:06:57,678 --> 00:06:59,503
the popular ones most of all.

131
00:07:01,444 --> 00:07:02,457
There's a catch.

132
00:07:02,457 --> 00:07:06,707
Suppose the grader is generous, and every answer earns at least two points.

133
00:07:06,707 --> 00:07:09,569
Then every sample pushes the policy toward itself;

134
00:07:09,569 --> 00:07:13,094
the pushes still average out to the right direction, but each estimate is

135
00:07:13,094 --> 00:07:14,332
mostly noise.

136
00:07:14,332 --> 00:07:17,744
Here are six runs of exactly the algorithm we just derived.

137
00:07:17,744 --> 00:07:20,119
They wander, and some never find the hill.

138
00:07:20,119 --> 00:07:21,582
Now make one change:

139
00:07:21,582 --> 00:07:24,807
subtract the batch's average reward before weighting.

140
00:07:24,807 --> 00:07:28,594
All six runs climb straight to the top.

141
00:07:29,069 --> 00:07:30,907
Why are we allowed to do that?

142
00:07:30,907 --> 00:07:35,119
Subtract any number b from the reward, and look at the expected gradient.

143
00:07:35,119 --> 00:07:39,644
It splits into the policy gradient, minus b times the expected score.

144
00:07:39,644 --> 00:07:42,769
And the expected score is zero, as we saw.

145
00:07:42,769 --> 00:07:47,007
So any baseline that doesn't depend on the sampled answer leaves the gradient

146
00:07:47,007 --> 00:07:48,957
unchanged on average.

147
00:07:48,957 --> 00:07:52,294
What it changes is the noise.

148
00:07:52,761 --> 00:07:54,649
Let's watch the noise directly.

149
00:07:54,649 --> 00:07:58,761
A policy over a two-dimensional action, on a landscape with two hills, plus

150
00:07:58,761 --> 00:08:00,549
the constant two points.

151
00:08:00,549 --> 00:08:02,561
Each sample suggests a direction:

152
00:08:02,561 --> 00:08:05,699
from the center toward itself, scaled by its reward.

153
00:08:05,699 --> 00:08:09,749
Average a batch of sixteen such suggestions and you get one gradient estimate,

154
00:08:09,749 --> 00:08:11,561
a single point on the right.

155
00:08:11,561 --> 00:08:13,736
Three hundred batches make this cloud.

156
00:08:13,736 --> 00:08:18,174
Its center is the true gradient, but the cloud is so wide that a single estimate

157
00:08:18,174 --> 00:08:20,586
often points the wrong way.

158
00:08:20,586 --> 00:08:22,924
Now subtract the average reward.

159
00:08:22,924 --> 00:08:25,711
Same center, but the cloud collapses:

160
00:08:25,711 --> 00:08:28,824
the variance drops by a factor of 27.

161
00:08:28,824 --> 00:08:30,711
The signal was always there;

162
00:08:30,711 --> 00:08:36,361
the baseline removes the part of the reward that every answer gets anyway.

163
00:08:36,778 --> 00:08:38,453
Which baseline is best?

164
00:08:38,453 --> 00:08:43,865
The variance is a quadratic in b, a parabola, so set its derivative to zero.

165
00:08:43,865 --> 00:08:48,065
The best baseline is the average reward, weighted by the squared size of the

166
00:08:48,065 --> 00:08:49,040
score.

167
00:08:49,040 --> 00:08:52,553
In practice that's very close to the plain average reward.

168
00:08:52,553 --> 00:08:56,303
Here, they agree to within a percent.

169
00:08:57,769 --> 00:08:58,957
One more step.

170
00:08:58,957 --> 00:09:01,557
Different prompts deserve different baselines:

171
00:09:01,557 --> 00:09:05,557
a policy that usually solves an easy problem shouldn't get excited about solving

172
00:09:05,557 --> 00:09:06,569
it again.

173
00:09:06,569 --> 00:09:10,894
So subtract V of x, the average reward the policy gets on this particular

174
00:09:10,894 --> 00:09:11,832
prompt.

175
00:09:11,832 --> 00:09:13,994
What's left is called the advantage:

176
00:09:13,994 --> 00:09:16,744
was this answer better or worse than usual?

177
00:09:16,744 --> 00:09:20,994
With a reward of one for right and zero for wrong, V is just the chance p

178
00:09:20,994 --> 00:09:22,494
of being right.

179
00:09:22,494 --> 00:09:27,007
On an easy prompt, a right answer earns a tiny plus zero point one, and a

180
00:09:27,007 --> 00:09:29,894
mistake a big minus zero point nine.

181
00:09:29,894 --> 00:09:32,032
On a hard prompt it's the reverse:

182
00:09:32,032 --> 00:09:34,769
a rare success is worth a lot.

183
00:09:34,769 --> 00:09:39,119
But to compute an advantage, we need V, and nobody hands it to us.

184
00:09:39,119 --> 00:09:42,994
And when the answer is a long sequence of tokens with a single reward at the

185
00:09:42,994 --> 00:09:45,594
end, a deeper question appears:

186
00:09:45,594 --> 00:09:47,444
which tokens deserve the credit?

187
00:09:49,267 --> 00:09:54,117
Here is a real answer from the model we'll train later, to the problem 5675

188
00:09:54,117 --> 00:09:56,242
plus 5563.

189
00:09:56,242 --> 00:10:00,705
It's wrong, so the reward is zero, and the policy gradient pushes every one

190
00:10:00,705 --> 00:10:02,742
of its tokens down equally.

191
00:10:02,742 --> 00:10:04,805
But most of those digits were fine.

192
00:10:04,805 --> 00:10:06,417
Only one was a mistake:

193
00:10:06,417 --> 00:10:07,855
a forgotten carry.

194
00:10:07,855 --> 00:10:12,767
A single reward at the end can't say which token earned it.

195
00:10:13,234 --> 00:10:16,047
The fix is to measure progress along the way.

196
00:10:16,047 --> 00:10:18,309
Define the value of a partial answer:

197
00:10:18,309 --> 00:10:23,109
the expected final reward, given the prompt and the tokens written so far.

198
00:10:23,109 --> 00:10:27,472
With a zero-one reward, it's simply the chance of ending up right from here.

199
00:10:27,472 --> 00:10:31,709
For a language model, nothing random happens between tokens except the next

200
00:10:31,709 --> 00:10:36,747
token itself, so the value now is the average value after one more token.

201
00:10:36,747 --> 00:10:38,784
That's the Bellman equation.

202
00:10:38,784 --> 00:10:41,322
And it hands us exactly what we wanted:

203
00:10:41,322 --> 00:10:44,797
a token's advantage is how much it changed the odds.

204
00:10:44,797 --> 00:10:48,009
The value after it, minus the value before.

205
00:10:48,009 --> 00:10:52,134
With an estimated value function, this difference is called the temporal difference

206
00:10:52,134 --> 00:10:54,909
error, delta.

207
00:10:55,359 --> 00:10:57,922
We can't compute V exactly, so we learn it:

208
00:10:57,922 --> 00:11:01,559
a second network, the critic, trained to predict the final reward from every

209
00:11:01,559 --> 00:11:03,822
prefix of the model's own answers.

210
00:11:03,822 --> 00:11:06,859
Here is a real one, reading three real answers.

211
00:11:06,859 --> 00:11:11,222
Before the model writes anything, the critic gives it a 58 percent chance.

212
00:11:11,222 --> 00:11:15,734
In this first answer the model starts by showing its work, and the value jumps:

213
00:11:15,734 --> 00:11:19,522
the critic has learned that worked answers are almost always right.

214
00:11:19,522 --> 00:11:22,834
Every later token barely changes anything.

215
00:11:22,834 --> 00:11:25,747
This one answers directly, and gets it right.

216
00:11:25,747 --> 00:11:30,734
Each digit nudges the value a little, as the carries go by without a slip.

217
00:11:30,734 --> 00:11:32,622
And here's our wrong answer.

218
00:11:32,622 --> 00:11:35,209
The value drops at exactly one token:

219
00:11:35,209 --> 00:11:37,497
the digit where the carry was forgotten.

220
00:11:37,497 --> 00:11:42,209
That negative delta is the credit assignment we wanted.

221
00:11:43,717 --> 00:11:48,817
Using delta alone trusts the critic completely, and the critic is only a guess.

222
00:11:48,817 --> 00:11:52,880
Generalized advantage estimation blends in the actual outcome:

223
00:11:52,880 --> 00:11:56,792
add up the deltas from here to the end, discounting each step by a factor

224
00:11:56,792 --> 00:11:57,742
lambda.

225
00:11:57,742 --> 00:12:01,142
With lambda zero, you get the critic's one-step opinion.

226
00:12:01,142 --> 00:12:05,292
With lambda one, the sum telescopes into the final reward minus the value

227
00:12:05,292 --> 00:12:05,930
now:

228
00:12:05,930 --> 00:12:08,417
unbiased, but noisier.

229
00:12:08,417 --> 00:12:11,905
On our wrong answer, slide lambda from zero to one.

230
00:12:11,905 --> 00:12:13,842
Almost nothing changes.

231
00:12:13,842 --> 00:12:15,267
This critic is good:

232
00:12:15,267 --> 00:12:19,292
the value already collapsed at the bad digit, so the later tokens owe nothing

233
00:12:19,292 --> 00:12:20,876
either way.

234
00:12:20,876 --> 00:12:26,601
Now take the critic away and use one number per prompt, the way GRPO and RLOO

235
00:12:26,601 --> 00:12:27,513
do.

236
00:12:27,513 --> 00:12:31,976
Every token gets the same blame, the correct digits included.

237
00:12:31,976 --> 00:12:34,601
And on a right answer that showed its work:

238
00:12:34,601 --> 00:12:38,363
the critic puts nearly all the credit on the single token that decided to

239
00:12:38,363 --> 00:12:39,601
show the work.

240
00:12:39,601 --> 00:12:44,613
Without a critic, that decision is just one token among thirteen, all sharing

241
00:12:44,613 --> 00:12:46,467
the same reward.

242
00:12:46,467 --> 00:12:51,092
The price is a critic network as large as the policy itself, trained alongside

243
00:12:51,092 --> 00:12:51,780
it.

244
00:12:51,780 --> 00:12:56,267
The algorithm behind today's reasoning models decided that price wasn't worth

245
00:12:56,267 --> 00:12:56,967
paying.

246
00:13:02,556 --> 00:13:06,956
So far, every gradient step used fresh samples from the current policy.

247
00:13:06,956 --> 00:13:12,043
But for a language model, generating the samples is by far the expensive part.

248
00:13:12,043 --> 00:13:16,006
A long answer is written one token at a time, while the gradient step that

249
00:13:16,006 --> 00:13:19,093
consumes it is a single parallel pass.

250
00:13:19,093 --> 00:13:22,843
So we'd like to squeeze several gradient steps out of each batch.

251
00:13:22,843 --> 00:13:26,556
The trouble is that after the first step, the batch was written by an older

252
00:13:26,556 --> 00:13:28,906
policy.

253
00:13:29,352 --> 00:13:32,852
There's a standard fix for data drawn from the wrong distribution:

254
00:13:32,852 --> 00:13:34,389
importance sampling.

255
00:13:34,389 --> 00:13:38,552
An average under the new policy is a sum weighted by its probabilities.

256
00:13:38,552 --> 00:13:43,052
Multiply and divide by the old policy's probability, and it becomes an average

257
00:13:43,052 --> 00:13:47,202
under the old policy, with each sample reweighted by the ratio rho of new

258
00:13:47,202 --> 00:13:48,939
to old probability.

259
00:13:48,939 --> 00:13:53,589
Apply that to the advantage and you get the surrogate objective that PPO and

260
00:13:53,589 --> 00:13:56,427
GRPO both optimize.

261
00:13:56,427 --> 00:14:01,227
At the old parameters the ratio is one, and its gradient is exactly the policy

262
00:14:01,227 --> 00:14:03,527
gradient.

263
00:14:03,968 --> 00:14:07,743
For a language model, the ratio is taken token by token.

264
00:14:07,743 --> 00:14:11,781
The exact ratio for a whole answer would be the product of all of them, and

265
00:14:11,781 --> 00:14:16,268
a product of hundreds of numbers near one can explode, so instead each token

266
00:14:16,268 --> 00:14:18,168
is reweighted on its own.

267
00:14:18,168 --> 00:14:19,581
How wrong is that?

268
00:14:19,581 --> 00:14:23,668
A classic identity, the performance difference lemma, says the improvement

269
00:14:23,668 --> 00:14:28,518
from one policy to another is the sum of the old policy's advantages, along

270
00:14:28,518 --> 00:14:30,918
trajectories of the new policy.

271
00:14:30,918 --> 00:14:33,406
The surrogate makes one approximation:

272
00:14:33,406 --> 00:14:36,531
it uses the prefixes the old policy visited.

273
00:14:36,531 --> 00:14:42,293
That's exact when the policies match, and good only while they stay close.

274
00:14:42,760 --> 00:14:46,385
Here's the picture, along one direction in parameter space.

275
00:14:46,385 --> 00:14:48,910
The true objective rises, then falls.

276
00:14:48,910 --> 00:14:52,472
The surrogate touches it at the old parameters and has the same slope, but

277
00:14:52,472 --> 00:14:54,810
it keeps promising more the further you go.

278
00:14:54,810 --> 00:14:57,097
Follow it blindly and you overshoot.

279
00:14:57,097 --> 00:15:01,822
TRPO proved that subtracting a multiple of the largest KL divergence between

280
00:15:01,822 --> 00:15:06,010
old and new policies gives a lower bound on the true objective.

281
00:15:06,010 --> 00:15:10,285
Maximize the bound instead, and every step is guaranteed not to make things

282
00:15:10,285 --> 00:15:12,435
worse.

283
00:15:12,877 --> 00:15:16,214
TRPO's constraint leads to a beautiful idea.

284
00:15:16,214 --> 00:15:19,764
Picture a policy over just three actions, A, B, and C.

285
00:15:19,764 --> 00:15:22,977
Every point of this triangle is a probability distribution;

286
00:15:22,977 --> 00:15:24,664
the corners are certainty.

287
00:15:24,664 --> 00:15:26,614
Action A pays the most.

288
00:15:26,614 --> 00:15:30,952
Now draw every distribution within the same small KL divergence of a few starting

289
00:15:30,952 --> 00:15:31,889
points.

290
00:15:31,889 --> 00:15:36,077
The regions aren't circles, and they shrink near the edges, where small probabilities

291
00:15:36,077 --> 00:15:37,514
become precious.

292
00:15:37,514 --> 00:15:42,572
KL measures distance between distributions, not between parameters.

293
00:15:42,572 --> 00:15:46,910
To second order, KL is a quadratic form in the parameter step, built from

294
00:15:46,910 --> 00:15:48,972
the Fisher information matrix.

295
00:15:48,972 --> 00:15:52,247
Steepest ascent measured this way is the natural gradient:

296
00:15:52,247 --> 00:15:56,310
the ordinary gradient, multiplied by the inverse Fisher matrix.

297
00:15:56,310 --> 00:15:59,522
Here are both, followed exactly from the same start.

298
00:15:59,522 --> 00:16:01,410
The ordinary gradient crawls:

299
00:16:01,410 --> 00:16:05,522
it moves each logit in proportion to its action's probability, so the rare

300
00:16:05,522 --> 00:16:08,197
best action barely moves at first.

301
00:16:08,197 --> 00:16:12,435
The natural gradient moves every logit by its advantage, and heads almost

302
00:16:12,435 --> 00:16:14,664
straight for the best corner.

303
00:16:14,664 --> 00:16:19,039
TRPO takes natural-gradient steps with a KL budget.

304
00:16:19,039 --> 00:16:23,264
It works, but it needs second-order machinery that is painful at the scale

305
00:16:23,264 --> 00:16:24,827
of a language model.

306
00:16:24,827 --> 00:16:29,389
PPO, in 2017, replaced it with something much simpler.

307
00:16:31,170 --> 00:16:35,645
Proximal policy optimization keeps the surrogate, but takes away the incentive

308
00:16:35,645 --> 00:16:37,208
to move too far.

309
00:16:37,208 --> 00:16:41,858
Take the surrogate term, ratio times advantage, compute a second copy with

310
00:16:41,858 --> 00:16:46,158
the ratio clipped to between zero point eight and one point two, and keep

311
00:16:46,158 --> 00:16:49,070
the smaller of the two.

312
00:16:49,579 --> 00:16:51,904
Look at it as a function of the ratio.

313
00:16:51,904 --> 00:16:55,791
For a good answer, the objective rises with the ratio, so the gradient pushes

314
00:16:55,791 --> 00:16:59,966
the token's probability up, until the ratio passes one point two.

315
00:16:59,966 --> 00:17:02,791
Then the objective goes flat, and the gradient is zero:

316
00:17:02,791 --> 00:17:04,729
no reward for pushing further.

317
00:17:04,729 --> 00:17:06,904
For a bad answer it's the mirror image:

318
00:17:06,904 --> 00:17:10,529
push the probability down, until it has fallen by twenty percent.

319
00:17:10,529 --> 00:17:11,604
Note the minimum:

320
00:17:11,604 --> 00:17:14,279
in the direction that would hurt, there's no clipping.

321
00:17:14,279 --> 00:17:18,879
The objective is a pessimistic bound on the surrogate.

322
00:17:19,329 --> 00:17:22,966
In probability terms, the clip gives each token a window.

323
00:17:22,966 --> 00:17:27,779
A token the old policy chose with probability zero point nine can move anywhere

324
00:17:27,779 --> 00:17:30,554
from zero point seven two up to one.

325
00:17:30,554 --> 00:17:32,441
But the window is relative:

326
00:17:32,441 --> 00:17:36,879
a token at one percent can only rise to one point two percent in a round of

327
00:17:36,879 --> 00:17:37,991
updates.

328
00:17:37,991 --> 00:17:42,166
That asymmetry between likely and unlikely tokens will come back to haunt

329
00:17:42,166 --> 00:17:44,179
us.

330
00:17:44,629 --> 00:17:45,379
Does it matter?

331
00:17:45,379 --> 00:17:49,141
Here's a real test on the pocket model you'll meet properly in a few minutes.

332
00:17:49,141 --> 00:17:49,691
Teal:

333
00:17:49,691 --> 00:17:51,329
one gradient step per batch.

334
00:17:51,329 --> 00:17:53,891
After five steps it's at 75 percent.

335
00:17:53,891 --> 00:17:54,466
Red:

336
00:17:54,466 --> 00:17:56,666
sixteen steps per batch, without the clip.

337
00:17:56,666 --> 00:17:58,679
Reusing the data is far more efficient:

338
00:17:58,679 --> 00:18:00,929
93 percent after five steps.

339
00:18:00,929 --> 00:18:01,729
But it wobbles:

340
00:18:01,729 --> 00:18:04,979
around step 40, one seed falls back to 91.

341
00:18:04,979 --> 00:18:05,704
Green:

342
00:18:05,704 --> 00:18:07,866
the same sixteen steps, clipped.

343
00:18:07,866 --> 00:18:12,279
Just as fast, and it climbs steadily, to 99.1 percent.

344
00:18:12,279 --> 00:18:15,229
In a model this small the difference is modest;

345
00:18:15,229 --> 00:18:19,129
the point of the clip is that you can take many steps on one batch without

346
00:18:19,129 --> 00:18:22,504
worrying about how far they go.

347
00:18:23,662 --> 00:18:28,774
This is the algorithm behind InstructGPT and the original ChatGPT.

348
00:18:28,774 --> 00:18:30,062
Count the networks:

349
00:18:30,062 --> 00:18:35,037
the policy being trained, a frozen copy of where it started, a reward model,

350
00:18:35,037 --> 00:18:39,174
and the critic, each as large as the language model itself.

351
00:18:39,174 --> 00:18:43,087
Every token pays a small penalty for drifting from the reference, and the

352
00:18:43,087 --> 00:18:46,262
reward model's score arrives at the end.

353
00:18:46,262 --> 00:18:50,624
Two of those four networks exist to turn human judgment into a number.

354
00:18:50,624 --> 00:18:52,437
So where does that number come from?

355
00:18:57,993 --> 00:18:59,756
Arithmetic has a checker.

356
00:18:59,756 --> 00:19:02,318
Helpfulness, clarity and tone don't.

357
00:19:02,318 --> 00:19:03,893
But people can compare:

358
00:19:03,893 --> 00:19:07,956
show someone two answers to the same prompt, and ask which is better.

359
00:19:07,956 --> 00:19:12,393
How do we turn a pile of such choices into a reward?

360
00:19:12,877 --> 00:19:14,939
Model each judgment like this.

361
00:19:14,939 --> 00:19:19,364
Every answer has a hidden utility, and each time a person judges, they perceive

362
00:19:19,364 --> 00:19:22,627
it with some noise, and choose whichever looks better.

363
00:19:22,627 --> 00:19:26,877
If that noise follows a Gumbel distribution, the distribution of the largest

364
00:19:26,877 --> 00:19:31,452
of many small effects, then the difference of two independent noises is exactly

365
00:19:31,452 --> 00:19:32,677
logistic.

366
00:19:32,677 --> 00:19:37,277
So the probability of choosing A is the area to the left of the utility gap:

367
00:19:37,277 --> 00:19:40,060
the logistic sigmoid of the difference.

368
00:19:40,060 --> 00:19:43,710
This is the Bradley-Terry model, from 1952.

369
00:19:43,710 --> 00:19:46,997
Notice that only the difference of utilities matters.

370
00:19:46,997 --> 00:19:52,735
Add the same constant to every answer for a prompt, and nothing changes.

371
00:19:53,193 --> 00:19:56,081
So fit the utilities by maximum likelihood.

372
00:19:56,081 --> 00:19:59,718
The reward model is a copy of the language model with a single number as its

373
00:19:59,718 --> 00:20:04,131
output, trained so that the preferred answer's score beats the rejected one's

374
00:20:04,131 --> 00:20:05,518
through the sigmoid.

375
00:20:05,518 --> 00:20:09,581
And because only differences matter, the reward model can carry an arbitrary

376
00:20:09,581 --> 00:20:11,381
offset for each prompt.

377
00:20:11,381 --> 00:20:12,843
That's harmless for us:

378
00:20:12,843 --> 00:20:17,006
a per-prompt offset is just a baseline, and we've seen that baselines don't

379
00:20:17,006 --> 00:20:20,143
change the policy gradient.

380
00:20:20,568 --> 00:20:25,181
But a reward model is a learned approximation, and the policy is an optimizer.

381
00:20:25,181 --> 00:20:28,768
Push hard enough, and the policy finds answers the reward model loves and

382
00:20:28,768 --> 00:20:29,793
people don't:

383
00:20:29,793 --> 00:20:33,331
the proxy keeps rising while true quality peaks and falls.

384
00:20:33,331 --> 00:20:36,731
OpenAI measured exactly this shape in 2022.

385
00:20:36,731 --> 00:20:39,943
That's why RLHF keeps the policy on a leash:

386
00:20:39,943 --> 00:20:42,218
the KL penalty to the reference.

387
00:20:42,218 --> 00:20:43,981
What does that leash actually do?

388
00:20:43,981 --> 00:20:47,368
Remarkably, we can solve for the best policy exactly.

389
00:20:49,325 --> 00:20:52,963
Here is the objective RLHF actually optimizes:

390
00:20:52,963 --> 00:20:58,238
expected reward, minus beta times the KL divergence from the reference policy.

391
00:20:58,238 --> 00:21:00,975
Write the KL as a sum over answers.

392
00:21:00,975 --> 00:21:05,925
Now fold the reward into the logarithm, as the log of e to the r over beta.

393
00:21:05,925 --> 00:21:10,625
Normalize that tilted distribution, with a constant Z, and the whole objective

394
00:21:10,625 --> 00:21:15,425
becomes minus beta times a KL divergence to a single distribution, plus a

395
00:21:15,425 --> 00:21:16,963
constant.

396
00:21:16,963 --> 00:21:21,863
A KL divergence is never negative, and it's zero only when the two distributions

397
00:21:21,863 --> 00:21:22,700
match.

398
00:21:22,700 --> 00:21:25,763
So the best possible policy is exactly this one:

399
00:21:25,763 --> 00:21:29,763
the reference, reweighted by e to the reward over beta.

400
00:21:29,763 --> 00:21:32,025
No training needed to know the answer;

401
00:21:32,025 --> 00:21:35,488
training is just a way to get there.

402
00:21:36,988 --> 00:21:39,263
Let's apply it to a real distribution:

403
00:21:39,263 --> 00:21:43,063
the answers our pocket model gives to one problem, before any reinforcement

404
00:21:43,063 --> 00:21:44,063
learning.

405
00:21:44,063 --> 00:21:48,063
Green answers are right, red ones are wrong, and the long ones show their

406
00:21:48,063 --> 00:21:48,913
work.

407
00:21:48,913 --> 00:21:52,538
With a large beta, the leash is tight and nothing changes.

408
00:21:52,538 --> 00:21:57,025
As beta shrinks, every right answer gets multiplied by the same factor, and

409
00:21:57,025 --> 00:21:58,638
the wrong ones fade.

410
00:21:58,638 --> 00:22:03,171
At small beta, the optimal policy is essentially always right.

411
00:22:03,171 --> 00:22:05,396
Notice what the tilt can't do.

412
00:22:05,396 --> 00:22:09,521
Right answers keep the proportions the reference gave them, and an answer

413
00:22:09,521 --> 00:22:14,309
the reference would never write gets probability zero, whatever its reward.

414
00:22:14,309 --> 00:22:19,521
The optimum only reweights what the starting model could already say.

415
00:22:19,954 --> 00:22:24,129
Back on the triangle of three-action policies, the optimal policies for every

416
00:22:24,129 --> 00:22:28,504
beta trace out a single curve, from the reference toward the best corner.

417
00:22:28,504 --> 00:22:30,017
And here's a surprise:

418
00:22:30,017 --> 00:22:33,304
it's exactly the path the natural gradient followed.

419
00:22:33,304 --> 00:22:37,729
Natural gradient ascent on the logits adds a multiple of the reward, so after

420
00:22:37,729 --> 00:22:41,804
time t the policy is the reference tilted by e to the t r.

421
00:22:41,804 --> 00:22:46,854
Training longer plays the same role as loosening the leash.

422
00:22:47,321 --> 00:22:50,921
Averaged over many problems, the optimal policies give a frontier:

423
00:22:50,921 --> 00:22:54,859
the most reward you can possibly have at each distance from the starting model.

424
00:22:54,859 --> 00:22:58,746
Being right every time costs only 0.39 nats.

425
00:22:58,746 --> 00:23:03,571
Our actual training run, which had no KL penalty at all, sits below the frontier,

426
00:23:03,571 --> 00:23:05,371
as every policy must.

427
00:23:05,371 --> 00:23:09,309
It ends almost as accurate, but about twice as far from where it started as

428
00:23:09,309 --> 00:23:10,584
it needed to be:

429
00:23:10,584 --> 00:23:15,671
unlike the optimum, it also reshuffled its right answers.

430
00:23:16,363 --> 00:23:20,588
In practice the KL itself has to be estimated from samples, one token at a

431
00:23:20,588 --> 00:23:21,513
time.

432
00:23:21,513 --> 00:23:25,588
John Schulman's three estimators are now in nearly every RL library.

433
00:23:25,588 --> 00:23:29,750
The obvious one, minus the log ratio, is unbiased, but it's negative half

434
00:23:29,750 --> 00:23:31,575
the time and very noisy.

435
00:23:31,575 --> 00:23:35,663
Half the squared log ratio is always positive, but slightly biased.

436
00:23:35,663 --> 00:23:40,275
And k3 adds a term whose average is exactly zero, rho minus one:

437
00:23:40,275 --> 00:23:44,688
it stays unbiased, it's never negative, and it's far less noisy.

438
00:23:44,688 --> 00:23:49,288
For two close distributions, its spread is fourteen times smaller.

439
00:23:49,288 --> 00:23:50,550
One subtlety.

440
00:23:50,550 --> 00:23:54,188
An estimator with the right value can have the wrong gradient.

441
00:23:54,188 --> 00:23:59,625
GRPO puts k3 directly in its loss, and differentiating it pulls the policy

442
00:23:59,625 --> 00:24:03,313
toward the reversed KL, from reference to policy.

443
00:24:03,313 --> 00:24:08,138
DeepSeek's V3.2 corrects this by multiplying in the importance ratio.

444
00:24:08,138 --> 00:24:12,838
And many reasoning recipes skip the leash entirely, and set beta to zero.

445
00:24:14,714 --> 00:24:19,164
In 2023, a group at Stanford ran this formula backwards.

446
00:24:19,164 --> 00:24:23,927
If the optimal policy is the reference tilted by the reward, take logs, and

447
00:24:23,927 --> 00:24:25,677
solve for the reward.

448
00:24:25,677 --> 00:24:27,889
Any policy implies a reward:

449
00:24:27,889 --> 00:24:32,327
the log ratio of its probabilities to the reference's, times beta, plus a

450
00:24:32,327 --> 00:24:36,064
term that depends only on the prompt.

451
00:24:36,556 --> 00:24:40,781
Now plug that implied reward into the Bradley-Terry model of comparisons.

452
00:24:40,781 --> 00:24:44,381
The probability that the preferred answer wins is the sigmoid of the reward

453
00:24:44,381 --> 00:24:45,406
difference.

454
00:24:45,406 --> 00:24:49,531
Substitute, and the troublesome normalizer Z appears twice, with opposite

455
00:24:49,531 --> 00:24:50,531
signs.

456
00:24:50,531 --> 00:24:53,843
Both answers answer the same prompt, so it cancels.

457
00:24:53,843 --> 00:24:57,231
What's left depends only on the policy and the reference.

458
00:24:57,231 --> 00:25:01,768
Fit it by maximum likelihood, and you have Direct Preference Optimization:

459
00:25:01,768 --> 00:25:04,931
a simple classification loss on pairs.

460
00:25:04,931 --> 00:25:09,306
No reward model, no sampling, no critic.

461
00:25:09,731 --> 00:25:12,006
Its gradient is easy to read.

462
00:25:12,006 --> 00:25:16,593
Raise the log probability of the preferred answer, lower the rejected one,

463
00:25:16,593 --> 00:25:20,893
and weight the pair by a sigmoid of how badly the model's own implicit reward

464
00:25:20,893 --> 00:25:22,418
currently ranks it.

465
00:25:22,418 --> 00:25:24,843
Pairs it already gets right fade away;

466
00:25:24,843 --> 00:25:28,556
pairs it gets wrong get the full push.

467
00:25:28,989 --> 00:25:33,077
Here it is on the adder, from four thousand pairs of right and wrong answers,

468
00:25:33,077 --> 00:25:35,439
sampled once from the starting model.

469
00:25:35,439 --> 00:25:38,602
Accuracy climbs from 69 to 80 percent.

470
00:25:38,602 --> 00:25:40,064
Then it collapses.

471
00:25:40,064 --> 00:25:41,789
Look at what the loss is doing:

472
00:25:41,789 --> 00:25:45,489
it only cares about the gap between the preferred and the rejected answer,

473
00:25:45,489 --> 00:25:49,064
and it found that pushing both down widens the gap fastest.

474
00:25:49,064 --> 00:25:51,002
The probability doesn't vanish;

475
00:25:51,002 --> 00:25:54,689
it flows to answers that were in neither set, and the model starts writing

476
00:25:54,689 --> 00:25:56,139
nonsense.

477
00:25:56,139 --> 00:25:59,698
This failure has a name, likelihood displacement.

478
00:25:59,698 --> 00:26:04,260
The fix people use is to stop early, regularize, or keep sampling:

479
00:26:04,260 --> 00:26:08,148
train on the model's own fresh answers instead of a fixed set.

480
00:26:08,148 --> 00:26:11,760
Which is exactly what the reinforcement learning methods do.

481
00:26:11,760 --> 00:26:16,085
For reasoning, the field went back to sampling, and to a much simpler kind

482
00:26:16,085 --> 00:26:16,948
of reward.

483
00:26:22,479 --> 00:26:26,917
In 2024, DeepSeek's math model introduced the algorithm behind many of today's

484
00:26:26,917 --> 00:26:28,567
open reasoning models:

485
00:26:28,567 --> 00:26:31,517
group relative policy optimization, GRPO.

486
00:26:31,517 --> 00:26:34,529
Its idea is to replace the critic with something free.

487
00:26:34,529 --> 00:26:37,242
For each prompt, sample a group of answers.

488
00:26:37,242 --> 00:26:39,592
Here are 8 real ones from our pocket model.

489
00:26:39,592 --> 00:26:41,292
A checker grades each one:

490
00:26:41,292 --> 00:26:43,517
one if right, zero if wrong.

491
00:26:43,517 --> 00:26:47,017
The group's average reward is an estimate of the value of the prompt, the

492
00:26:47,017 --> 00:26:49,904
very thing the critic was trained to predict.

493
00:26:49,904 --> 00:26:54,329
Subtract it, and you have an advantage for every answer, with no value network

494
00:26:54,329 --> 00:26:56,479
at all.

495
00:26:56,903 --> 00:26:58,628
Is that baseline legal?

496
00:26:58,628 --> 00:27:02,815
It includes the answer itself, and a baseline isn't allowed to depend on the

497
00:27:02,815 --> 00:27:04,290
sampled answer.

498
00:27:04,290 --> 00:27:08,678
But split the average into answer i's own share and the average of the other

499
00:27:08,678 --> 00:27:10,053
answers.

500
00:27:10,053 --> 00:27:14,815
Rearranged, answer i minus the group mean is exactly a constant times answer

501
00:27:14,815 --> 00:27:19,140
i minus the average of the others, and that leave-one-out baseline is independent

502
00:27:19,140 --> 00:27:20,265
of answer i.

503
00:27:20,265 --> 00:27:25,090
So the group mean gives an unbiased gradient, just scaled by G minus one over

504
00:27:25,090 --> 00:27:25,715
G.

505
00:27:25,715 --> 00:27:31,053
This leave-one-out version is known as RLOO.

506
00:27:31,528 --> 00:27:33,790
GRPO does one more thing:

507
00:27:33,790 --> 00:27:36,565
it divides by the group's standard deviation.

508
00:27:36,565 --> 00:27:41,053
That looks like a harmless normalization, but it changes what's being optimized.

509
00:27:41,053 --> 00:27:45,278
With rewards of zero and one and a success rate p, the standard deviation

510
00:27:45,278 --> 00:27:50,228
is the square root of p times one minus p, so a right answer gets this advantage,

511
00:27:50,228 --> 00:27:51,803
and a wrong one this.

512
00:27:51,803 --> 00:27:56,328
Average over the samples, and the expected update is the gradient of p, divided

513
00:27:56,328 --> 00:27:58,503
by that same square root.

514
00:27:58,503 --> 00:28:01,853
And that's the derivative of two arcsine root p.

515
00:28:01,853 --> 00:28:05,628
So GRPO doesn't maximize the success rate itself.

516
00:28:05,628 --> 00:28:09,790
It maximizes the arcsine of its square root, which puts extra weight on prompts

517
00:28:09,790 --> 00:28:13,790
the model almost always fails or almost always solves.

518
00:28:13,790 --> 00:28:16,003
Here's that weight, as a function of p.

519
00:28:16,003 --> 00:28:17,965
With a finite group it's tamer:

520
00:28:17,965 --> 00:28:21,603
these are the exact curves for groups of two to sixty-four.

521
00:28:21,603 --> 00:28:25,378
With two answers, the normalization does nothing at all.

522
00:28:25,378 --> 00:28:27,865
And two arcsine root p has a meaning:

523
00:28:27,865 --> 00:28:31,565
it's the distance along the family of coin flips, measured with the Fisher

524
00:28:31,565 --> 00:28:35,528
information, the same geometry as the natural gradient.

525
00:28:35,528 --> 00:28:37,340
Some labs keep this weighting;

526
00:28:37,340 --> 00:28:41,140
others, like OLMo 3, DeepSeek-V3.2 and Dr.

527
00:28:41,140 --> 00:28:45,153
GRPO, drop the division.

528
00:28:45,574 --> 00:28:47,524
There's a catch hiding in the group.

529
00:28:47,524 --> 00:28:51,624
If every answer in a group is right, or every one is wrong, every advantage

530
00:28:51,624 --> 00:28:54,599
is zero, and the whole group teaches nothing.

531
00:28:54,599 --> 00:28:58,599
That happens with probability p to the G plus one minus p to the G.

532
00:28:58,599 --> 00:29:03,561
On easy prompts, late in training, most groups are wasted compute.

533
00:29:03,561 --> 00:29:07,974
ByteDance's DAPO filters these groups out and keeps sampling until the batch

534
00:29:07,974 --> 00:29:11,324
is full of informative ones.

535
00:29:11,865 --> 00:29:15,865
Now we can read the whole GRPO objective, the formula from the start of the

536
00:29:15,865 --> 00:29:16,778
video.

537
00:29:16,778 --> 00:29:19,665
Answers sampled by the old policy, in groups.

538
00:29:19,665 --> 00:29:24,340
The importance ratio, clipped as in PPO, times the group-relative advantage.

539
00:29:24,340 --> 00:29:27,640
And the leash to the reference model, estimated with k3.

540
00:29:27,640 --> 00:29:29,653
There's one piece we haven't explained:

541
00:29:29,653 --> 00:29:33,840
inside, each answer's tokens are averaged, one over the length of answer i.

542
00:29:33,840 --> 00:29:36,690
That innocent-looking average turns out to be a trap.

543
00:29:36,690 --> 00:29:40,440
But first, let's watch GRPO train a real model.

544
00:29:42,386 --> 00:29:43,536
Time to run it.

545
00:29:43,536 --> 00:29:45,798
We train the adder with GRPO:

546
00:29:45,798 --> 00:29:50,286
thirty-two problems per step, eight answers each, a reward of one or zero,

547
00:29:50,286 --> 00:29:51,698
and nothing else.

548
00:29:51,698 --> 00:29:56,448
Accuracy on problems it has never seen climbs from 70 percent to 98 percent

549
00:29:56,448 --> 00:29:57,986
within forty steps.

550
00:29:57,986 --> 00:29:58,936
But how?

551
00:29:58,936 --> 00:30:02,486
You might expect it to start showing its work, since that's almost always

552
00:30:02,486 --> 00:30:03,236
right.

553
00:30:03,236 --> 00:30:04,361
It barely does:

554
00:30:04,361 --> 00:30:07,611
from 28 to 41 percent of answers.

555
00:30:07,611 --> 00:30:10,536
Instead, its direct answers stopped slipping:

556
00:30:10,536 --> 00:30:13,673
from 59 to 97 percent right.

557
00:30:13,673 --> 00:30:17,711
Forgetting a carry was always a choice the network could unlearn, one token

558
00:30:17,711 --> 00:30:21,936
at a time, and that was the cheapest way up.

559
00:30:22,386 --> 00:30:27,173
Here are its five likeliest answers to our running example, before and after.

560
00:30:27,173 --> 00:30:31,336
Before, the right answer shared its probability with several slips.

561
00:30:31,336 --> 00:30:35,686
After, nearly all of it sits on the right answer, written directly.

562
00:30:35,686 --> 00:30:37,723
That's what RL did to this model:

563
00:30:37,723 --> 00:30:40,186
it sharpened.

564
00:30:40,811 --> 00:30:44,498
Now the same experiment with six of the algorithms we've derived, three seeds

565
00:30:44,498 --> 00:30:45,261
each.

566
00:30:45,261 --> 00:30:48,023
On accuracy, they look almost interchangeable.

567
00:30:48,023 --> 00:30:52,048
PPO starts late, because its critic has to be trained first, and every one

568
00:30:52,048 --> 00:30:54,336
of them ends above ninety-five percent.

569
00:30:54,336 --> 00:30:56,086
But look at what they learned to do.

570
00:30:56,086 --> 00:31:01,061
GRPO and RLOO end up showing their work about 42 percent of the time;

571
00:31:01,061 --> 00:31:02,073
DAPO, Dr.

572
00:31:02,073 --> 00:31:05,236
GRPO and PPO, about 50.

573
00:31:05,236 --> 00:31:09,036
And plain REINFORCE abandons showing its work completely.

574
00:31:09,036 --> 00:31:12,661
Same reward, same accuracy, different behavior.

575
00:31:12,661 --> 00:31:16,061
The difference turns out to hide in one innocent-looking average.

576
00:31:18,185 --> 00:31:19,310
Here's the trap.

577
00:31:19,310 --> 00:31:23,685
GRPO averages over an answer's tokens before averaging over the group, so

578
00:31:23,685 --> 00:31:27,347
each token's push is the advantage divided by the answer's length.

579
00:31:27,347 --> 00:31:29,910
Two right answers, one short and one long:

580
00:31:29,910 --> 00:31:32,547
the short one's tokens get pushed up twice as hard.

581
00:31:32,547 --> 00:31:33,785
Two wrong answers:

582
00:31:33,785 --> 00:31:36,522
the long one's tokens are punished half as much.

583
00:31:36,522 --> 00:31:40,210
So when the model is right, it learns to be brief, and when it's wrong, it

584
00:31:40,210 --> 00:31:42,697
learns that rambling dilutes the blame.

585
00:31:42,697 --> 00:31:46,585
Long, wrong answers are the result.

586
00:31:47,018 --> 00:31:51,268
Two 2025 papers removed the bias in different ways.

587
00:31:51,268 --> 00:31:56,143
ByteDance's DAPO averages over all tokens in the batch at once, so every token

588
00:31:56,143 --> 00:31:57,593
counts equally.

589
00:31:57,593 --> 00:31:58,056
Dr.

590
00:31:58,056 --> 00:32:04,143
GRPO divides by a fixed constant, the maximum length, and also drops the division

591
00:32:04,143 --> 00:32:10,768
by the standard deviation, arguing that both normalizations bias the gradient.

592
00:32:11,243 --> 00:32:14,356
In our pocket model, this bias isn't hypothetical.

593
00:32:14,356 --> 00:32:17,868
Remember plain REINFORCE, which abandoned showing its work?

594
00:32:17,868 --> 00:32:20,956
It used GRPO's per-answer average.

595
00:32:20,956 --> 00:32:25,268
Without a baseline, only right answers get pushed, and a right direct answer

596
00:32:25,268 --> 00:32:29,081
is six tokens long while a right worked answer is thirteen:

597
00:32:29,081 --> 00:32:33,368
per token, the short answers push twice as hard on the very first choice,

598
00:32:33,368 --> 00:32:35,393
and nothing pushes back.

599
00:32:35,393 --> 00:32:39,068
Replace that average with a constant and change nothing else:

600
00:32:39,068 --> 00:32:43,318
the same algorithm now ends up showing its work 50 percent of the time.

601
00:32:43,318 --> 00:32:46,843
With a baseline the effect is milder, but it's still there:

602
00:32:46,843 --> 00:32:51,331
GRPO, with the per-answer average, ends at 41 percent;

603
00:32:51,331 --> 00:32:52,356
DAPO and Dr.

604
00:32:52,356 --> 00:32:57,493
GRPO, without it, at 48 and 50.

605
00:32:58,251 --> 00:33:00,276
These choices aren't academic.

606
00:33:00,276 --> 00:33:03,514
We read the code of eleven open-source RL libraries;

607
00:33:03,514 --> 00:33:04,789
here are nine.

608
00:33:04,789 --> 00:33:07,264
They disagree on almost every one of them:

609
00:33:07,264 --> 00:33:11,901
how to average over tokens, whether to divide by the standard deviation, whether

610
00:33:11,901 --> 00:33:15,901
to keep a KL leash at all, and which estimator to use for it.

611
00:33:15,901 --> 00:33:21,826
When a paper says it used GRPO, it's naming a family of choices, not one algorithm.

612
00:33:24,009 --> 00:33:28,009
Every RL run on a language model shows the same signature.

613
00:33:28,009 --> 00:33:32,821
The policy's entropy, its average uncertainty about the next token, collapses.

614
00:33:32,821 --> 00:33:34,446
Here it is in our run.

615
00:33:34,446 --> 00:33:36,609
Entropy is the exploration budget:

616
00:33:36,609 --> 00:33:41,546
once the policy is certain, it stops sampling anything new, and learning stalls.

617
00:33:41,546 --> 00:33:45,234
Why does it fall, and can we control it?

618
00:33:45,680 --> 00:33:49,605
For a softmax, the derivative of the entropy with respect to a logit is minus

619
00:33:49,605 --> 00:33:54,280
that token's probability, times how much more surprising than average it is.

620
00:33:54,280 --> 00:33:58,242
Sum over a small change of all the logits, and the change in entropy is minus

621
00:33:58,242 --> 00:33:59,530
a covariance:

622
00:33:59,530 --> 00:34:04,105
between how likely each token already is, and how much its logit moves.

623
00:34:04,105 --> 00:34:08,217
Plug in the policy gradient, which moves each logit by its probability times

624
00:34:08,217 --> 00:34:13,555
its advantage, or the natural gradient, which moves it by the advantage alone.

625
00:34:13,555 --> 00:34:14,480
Either way:

626
00:34:14,480 --> 00:34:18,455
the entropy falls whenever the tokens the model already likes are the ones

627
00:34:18,455 --> 00:34:21,080
being rewarded.

628
00:34:21,538 --> 00:34:25,388
Two examples, each with the same five-token distribution.

629
00:34:25,388 --> 00:34:29,350
On the left, the token the model already likes is the one that gets rewarded:

630
00:34:29,350 --> 00:34:31,213
a positive covariance.

631
00:34:31,213 --> 00:34:34,613
One natural gradient step, and the distribution sharpens;

632
00:34:34,613 --> 00:34:36,088
the entropy drops.

633
00:34:36,088 --> 00:34:38,663
On the right, a rare token is rewarded.

634
00:34:38,663 --> 00:34:42,500
The covariance is negative, and the step spreads probability out:

635
00:34:42,500 --> 00:34:44,313
entropy rises.

636
00:34:44,313 --> 00:34:49,313
In RL on a pretrained model, the first case is overwhelmingly common:

637
00:34:49,313 --> 00:34:53,763
the confident tokens are usually the right ones, so entropy steadily drains

638
00:34:53,763 --> 00:34:55,913
away.

639
00:34:56,380 --> 00:35:00,430
Does the formula hold up in a real network, where every logit is tied to every

640
00:35:00,430 --> 00:35:02,167
other through shared weights?

641
00:35:02,167 --> 00:35:05,992
Here is the covariance between each sampled token's log probability and its

642
00:35:05,992 --> 00:35:10,267
advantage, measured in every batch of our run, and here is how much the entropy

643
00:35:10,267 --> 00:35:11,742
actually changed.

644
00:35:11,742 --> 00:35:16,355
The covariance is positive in 87 percent of the batches, and the entropy keeps

645
00:35:16,355 --> 00:35:17,380
falling.

646
00:35:17,380 --> 00:35:21,567
Averaged over a few steps, the bigger the covariance, the faster the fall:

647
00:35:21,567 --> 00:35:25,580
a correlation of minus 0.8.

648
00:35:26,013 --> 00:35:27,838
Remember the clipping windows.

649
00:35:27,838 --> 00:35:32,125
With a symmetric clip, a rare token can rise by at most twenty percent of

650
00:35:32,125 --> 00:35:37,100
its probability per round, while a likely one can rise all the way to certainty.

651
00:35:37,100 --> 00:35:40,013
That's exactly the positive covariance again:

652
00:35:40,013 --> 00:35:42,613
the clip itself favors sharpening.

653
00:35:42,613 --> 00:35:47,250
ByteDance's DAPO simply raises the upper limit to one point two eight, giving

654
00:35:47,250 --> 00:35:49,763
rare tokens more room to grow.

655
00:35:49,763 --> 00:35:54,963
Along with dynamic sampling, a token-level loss and a soft penalty for overlong

656
00:35:54,963 --> 00:36:00,763
answers, it took a naive GRPO run on the AIME math competition from thirty

657
00:36:00,763 --> 00:36:01,963
to fifty percent.

658
00:36:03,882 --> 00:36:07,207
Does reinforcement learning teach a model new things, or just make it more

659
00:36:07,207 --> 00:36:09,370
reliable at things it could already do?

660
00:36:09,370 --> 00:36:12,007
To ask that precisely, we need pass at k:

661
00:36:12,007 --> 00:36:14,557
the chance that at least one of k attempts is right.

662
00:36:14,557 --> 00:36:18,445
With a success rate p, it's one minus the chance that all k fail.

663
00:36:18,445 --> 00:36:21,832
To estimate it, draw n samples, count the right ones, and ask:

664
00:36:21,832 --> 00:36:25,282
of all the ways to pick k of them, what fraction contain no right answer at

665
00:36:25,282 --> 00:36:25,970
all?

666
00:36:25,970 --> 00:36:29,945
Here, ten of the fifty-six ways fail, so pass at three is about point eight

667
00:36:29,945 --> 00:36:32,007
two.

668
00:36:32,482 --> 00:36:37,307
Here is pass at k for our adder, measured with 128 samples on each of 300

669
00:36:37,307 --> 00:36:38,770
held-out problems.

670
00:36:38,770 --> 00:36:43,945
Before RL, a single attempt is right 70 percent of the time, but with 128

671
00:36:43,945 --> 00:36:47,895
attempts, every one of the problems gets solved at least once.

672
00:36:47,895 --> 00:36:52,545
After RL, a single attempt is right 99 percent of the time.

673
00:36:52,545 --> 00:36:54,045
The curve went flat:

674
00:36:54,045 --> 00:36:58,657
RL moved the probability onto answers the model could already produce.

675
00:36:58,657 --> 00:37:04,107
Its mistakes were slips, and the right answer was always within reach.

676
00:37:04,595 --> 00:37:06,220
This matches the math.

677
00:37:06,220 --> 00:37:11,270
The KL-regularized optimum is the reference times a positive factor, so any

678
00:37:11,270 --> 00:37:15,307
answer the reference would never produce stays at probability zero.

679
00:37:15,307 --> 00:37:20,632
In 2025, a careful study found the same thing in large reasoning models:

680
00:37:20,632 --> 00:37:25,032
given enough attempts, the base model catches up with, and even passes, the

681
00:37:25,032 --> 00:37:27,045
RL-trained one.

682
00:37:27,045 --> 00:37:32,407
NVIDIA's ProRL pushed back, finding problems that only prolonged RL could

683
00:37:32,407 --> 00:37:33,307
solve;

684
00:37:33,307 --> 00:37:38,582
and in practice, generalization lets a network reach answers it never sampled.

685
00:37:38,582 --> 00:37:43,320
As of 2026, the question is still open.

686
00:37:43,765 --> 00:37:45,978
Other measurements point the same way.

687
00:37:45,978 --> 00:37:50,278
A reward of zero or one carries about one bit of information per attempt,

688
00:37:50,278 --> 00:37:55,415
so even DeepSeek's R1-Zero received only a few million bits, fewer than the

689
00:37:55,415 --> 00:37:57,890
parameters of a tiny adapter.

690
00:37:57,890 --> 00:37:59,890
Most weights don't change at all.

691
00:37:59,890 --> 00:38:04,340
And on-policy RL tends to find the solution closest to the starting model,

692
00:38:04,340 --> 00:38:09,890
which is why it forgets less than fine-tuning on someone else's answers.

693
00:38:10,311 --> 00:38:14,286
If one bit per attempt is the bottleneck, there's a fix when a stronger model

694
00:38:14,286 --> 00:38:15,436
exists.

695
00:38:15,436 --> 00:38:20,111
Sample from the student, as in RL, but let a teacher grade every token:

696
00:38:20,111 --> 00:38:23,999
the reward for each token is how much more likely the teacher finds it.

697
00:38:23,999 --> 00:38:28,111
That's on-policy distillation, the same log-derivative trick with a dense

698
00:38:28,111 --> 00:38:29,111
reward.

699
00:38:29,111 --> 00:38:34,186
For Qwen3's eight-billion-parameter model it beat RL on the AIME benchmark

700
00:38:34,186 --> 00:38:36,086
for a tenth of the compute.

701
00:38:36,086 --> 00:38:41,836
And in April 2026, DeepSeek's V4 replaced its final RL stage with exactly

702
00:38:41,836 --> 00:38:45,911
this, distilling from specialist teachers that had each been trained with

703
00:38:45,911 --> 00:38:47,286
GRPO.

704
00:38:52,789 --> 00:38:56,502
At scale, generation and training run in different software.

705
00:38:56,502 --> 00:39:02,077
An inference engine like vLLM writes the answers, with kernels tuned for speed.

706
00:39:02,077 --> 00:39:05,914
A training framework computes the gradients, with different kernels and often

707
00:39:05,914 --> 00:39:07,302
different precision.

708
00:39:07,302 --> 00:39:11,302
They hold the same weights, but they don't compute the same probabilities.

709
00:39:11,302 --> 00:39:16,264
So even a perfectly on-policy algorithm is quietly off-policy, and the importance

710
00:39:16,264 --> 00:39:20,289
ratio that should be exactly one isn't.

711
00:39:20,689 --> 00:39:21,727
We measured it.

712
00:39:21,727 --> 00:39:27,289
A 0.6-billion-parameter Qwen3 model wrote eight reasoning traces, sampled

713
00:39:27,289 --> 00:39:31,889
token by token in bfloat16, the way an inference engine runs.

714
00:39:31,889 --> 00:39:36,739
Then we re-scored exactly the same tokens in a single float32 pass, the way

715
00:39:36,739 --> 00:39:38,064
a trainer might.

716
00:39:38,064 --> 00:39:41,627
Most log ratios are tiny, but the tails are wide:

717
00:39:41,627 --> 00:39:45,639
25 percent of tokens differ by more than one percent.

718
00:39:45,639 --> 00:39:50,952
Re-scoring in the same bfloat16, just in one pass instead of token by token,

719
00:39:50,952 --> 00:39:52,052
is no better:

720
00:39:52,052 --> 00:39:53,514
24 percent.

721
00:39:53,514 --> 00:39:56,914
It's the order of the arithmetic, not only the precision.

722
00:39:56,914 --> 00:40:00,064
And the disagreement lives on the unlikely tokens.

723
00:40:00,064 --> 00:40:04,989
Near-certain tokens almost never move, but 92 percent of tokens the sampler

724
00:40:04,989 --> 00:40:09,077
gave less than a ten percent chance are off by more than one percent:

725
00:40:09,077 --> 00:40:13,889
exactly the rare choices that exploration depends on.

726
00:40:14,397 --> 00:40:16,147
Now look at whole answers.

727
00:40:16,147 --> 00:40:20,447
The exact importance weight of a sequence is the product of its token ratios,

728
00:40:20,447 --> 00:40:22,335
so its logarithm is a sum:

729
00:40:22,335 --> 00:40:23,760
a random walk.

730
00:40:23,760 --> 00:40:26,060
Here are the walks for our eight traces.

731
00:40:26,060 --> 00:40:29,235
They wander away from zero as the answers get longer:

732
00:40:29,235 --> 00:40:36,247
after 768 tokens, one answer's weight is 8.5 and another's is 0.48, though

733
00:40:36,247 --> 00:40:38,722
not one weight of the model changed.

734
00:40:38,722 --> 00:40:44,460
Qwen's GSPO uses the geometric mean instead, the average log ratio, which

735
00:40:44,460 --> 00:40:48,622
shrinks back toward zero as the answer grows.

736
00:40:50,006 --> 00:40:54,468
Since 2025 the field has converged on a handful of fixes.

737
00:40:54,468 --> 00:40:58,981
Truncated importance sampling multiplies each token's gradient by its sampler-to-learner

738
00:40:58,981 --> 00:41:01,381
ratio, capped at a constant.

739
00:41:01,381 --> 00:41:06,456
Masking simply drops tokens or sequences whose ratios are too extreme.

740
00:41:06,456 --> 00:41:11,768
GSPO uses one ratio per answer, and for mixture-of-experts models replays

741
00:41:11,768 --> 00:41:16,231
the experts the sampler used, since about one expert in ten changes after

742
00:41:16,231 --> 00:41:17,756
each update.

743
00:41:17,756 --> 00:41:22,693
MiniMax's CISPO caps the importance weight but never zeroes a token's gradient,

744
00:41:22,693 --> 00:41:27,506
so rare reflective tokens like wait, or however, keep contributing.

745
00:41:27,506 --> 00:41:29,718
And some just fix the arithmetic:

746
00:41:29,718 --> 00:41:35,331
a float32 output layer, or float16 rollouts.

747
00:41:35,806 --> 00:41:38,806
Staleness is the other source of off-policy data.

748
00:41:38,806 --> 00:41:41,618
Long answers make generation the bottleneck:

749
00:41:41,618 --> 00:41:46,006
in OLMo 3's runs the learner spent three quarters of its time waiting.

750
00:41:46,006 --> 00:41:50,356
So large systems decouple the two, generating with slightly old weights while

751
00:41:50,356 --> 00:41:54,956
training continues, and stream new weights to the generators as they go.

752
00:41:54,956 --> 00:41:59,293
Every one of these importance-ratio corrections now has to absorb that lag

753
00:41:59,293 --> 00:41:59,906
too.

754
00:42:01,946 --> 00:42:03,984
How much compute goes into this?

755
00:42:03,984 --> 00:42:10,046
In 2022, InstructGPT's reinforcement learning cost under two percent of GPT-3's

756
00:42:10,046 --> 00:42:11,346
pretraining.

757
00:42:11,346 --> 00:42:14,596
DeepSeek's R1-Zero, a few percent.

758
00:42:14,596 --> 00:42:20,596
DeepSeek-V3.2 reported a post-training budget above ten percent of pretraining.

759
00:42:20,596 --> 00:42:26,059
xAI described Grok 4's reinforcement learning as running at pretraining scale,

760
00:42:26,059 --> 00:42:31,521
and in February 2026 Cursor reported that post-training its coding model took

761
00:42:31,521 --> 00:42:36,471
more compute than pretraining the base model it started from.

762
00:42:36,954 --> 00:42:42,154
In late 2025, a Meta team spent more than four hundred thousand GPU hours

763
00:42:42,154 --> 00:42:46,279
studying how RL improves with compute, and found a law.

764
00:42:46,279 --> 00:42:50,892
Accuracy follows a sigmoid in the logarithm of compute, with three parameters:

765
00:42:50,892 --> 00:42:56,004
a ceiling A, an efficiency exponent B, and the compute needed to get halfway.

766
00:42:56,004 --> 00:42:59,629
Fit it on small runs, and it predicts large ones.

767
00:42:59,629 --> 00:43:02,354
Here it is fitted to our own pocket-sized runs:

768
00:43:02,354 --> 00:43:07,942
GRPO taking one, four or sixteen gradient steps per batch, three seeds each.

769
00:43:07,942 --> 00:43:10,054
All three head for the same ceiling;

770
00:43:10,054 --> 00:43:13,279
what changes is how much compute it takes to get there.

771
00:43:13,279 --> 00:43:15,717
That was their key finding at scale too:

772
00:43:15,717 --> 00:43:20,354
most tricks change how fast you climb, not the ceiling.

773
00:43:20,796 --> 00:43:22,996
The same math now trains agents.

774
00:43:22,996 --> 00:43:26,071
An episode is a long conversation with an environment:

775
00:43:26,071 --> 00:43:30,146
the model calls tools, reads their output, and acts again, and a test suite

776
00:43:30,146 --> 00:43:32,109
decides the reward at the end.

777
00:43:32,109 --> 00:43:36,084
Only the model's own tokens are actions, so the tool outputs are masked out

778
00:43:36,084 --> 00:43:37,234
of the gradient.

779
00:43:37,234 --> 00:43:39,734
Everything else is the log-derivative trick.

780
00:43:39,734 --> 00:43:41,434
What changed is scale:

781
00:43:41,434 --> 00:43:45,934
labs now build environments by the thousands, and in 2026 by the hundreds

782
00:43:45,934 --> 00:43:47,134
of thousands.

783
00:43:47,134 --> 00:43:50,521
Each one is a reward function.

784
00:43:50,971 --> 00:43:55,921
And every reward function is a specification that an optimizer will read literally.

785
00:43:55,921 --> 00:44:00,321
If an object that claims to equal everything makes the tests pass, RL will

786
00:44:00,321 --> 00:44:01,421
find it.

787
00:44:01,421 --> 00:44:06,459
Anthropic reported in 2025 that models which learned such hacks in real coding

788
00:44:06,459 --> 00:44:11,459
environments generalized to broader misbehavior, including sabotage;

789
00:44:11,459 --> 00:44:14,971
a one-line framing in the prompt cut most of that.

790
00:44:14,971 --> 00:44:19,446
OpenAI found that reading the model's chain of thought catches most hacks,

791
00:44:19,446 --> 00:44:24,771
but that training against that monitor teaches the model to hide them.

792
00:44:25,188 --> 00:44:28,563
Where does that leave things, in October 2026?

793
00:44:28,563 --> 00:44:32,038
Some labs now get part of what RL gave them more cheaply:

794
00:44:32,038 --> 00:44:37,188
DeepSeek's V4 replaced its final mixed reinforcement learning stage with on-policy

795
00:44:37,188 --> 00:44:41,788
distillation from specialist teachers, themselves trained with GRPO.

796
00:44:41,788 --> 00:44:45,350
Others run RL continuously from deployment.

797
00:44:45,350 --> 00:44:49,363
Rewards increasingly come from rubrics graded by models.

798
00:44:49,363 --> 00:44:54,238
And the deepest question, whether RL teaches models new skills or only sharpens

799
00:44:54,238 --> 00:44:57,313
old ones, is still argued in both directions.

800
00:44:59,336 --> 00:45:01,736
Let's put the whole family in one place.

801
00:45:01,736 --> 00:45:03,836
It all starts from one identity:

802
00:45:03,836 --> 00:45:08,561
the gradient of an expected reward is the expected reward times the score.

803
00:45:08,561 --> 00:45:13,086
Subtract a baseline, and the noise falls while the mean stays put.

804
00:45:13,086 --> 00:45:17,236
Learn a value function, and each token gets its own credit.

805
00:45:17,236 --> 00:45:21,424
Reweight old samples by an importance ratio and clip it, and you can reuse

806
00:45:21,424 --> 00:45:23,074
every batch safely:

807
00:45:23,074 --> 00:45:24,149
that's PPO.

808
00:45:24,149 --> 00:45:28,486
Add a leash to the reference, and the optimal policy has a closed form, which

809
00:45:28,486 --> 00:45:29,611
hands you DPO.

810
00:45:29,611 --> 00:45:33,461
Replace the critic with a group of samples, and you have GRPO.

811
00:45:33,461 --> 00:45:38,011
Fix how it averages over tokens, tame its ratios when sampler and learner

812
00:45:38,011 --> 00:45:42,374
disagree, and if you have a better model to learn from, give every token its

813
00:45:42,374 --> 00:45:44,086
own reward.

814
00:45:44,086 --> 00:45:48,349
Every one of these is the log-derivative trick, plus one idea about noise

815
00:45:48,349 --> 00:45:49,824
or about trust.

816
00:45:49,824 --> 00:45:55,086
A model samples, a checker says one or zero, and those single bits, multiplied

817
00:45:55,086 --> 00:45:59,986
across millions of attempts, add up to a model that reasons far more reliably.
